lisa-design:
- Scope split BCH-1316 vs BCH-1330: cedar is OPT-IN (CCF_AUTHZ_DRIVER=cedar); the DEFAULT stays `builtin` so this PR is zero-behavior-change. Flipping the default to cedar is explicitly BCH-1330 — do NOT change DefaultAuthzDriver here.
- Bundled role policies are GENERATED from the manifest roles: block at startup (CompileRolePolicies/renderRolePolicies), not shipped as a static .cedar file. The manifest is the single source of truth; editing roles means editing manifest.yaml. Output is deterministic (sorted) for golden tests.
- Cedar RBAC is deny-by-default: a subject with no assigned role is denied everything. This DIFFERS from builtin (authenticated=allowed) but only applies when cedar is selected — intentional, it is real RBAC.
- Group-based assignment is wired but INERT until BCH-1328 populates subject.Props["groups"] (native ∪ SSO groups). User-based (by email) assignment is fully live now. Deliberately did NOT build an SSO/DB groups resolver here — that surface is BCH-1328's responsibility; subjectGroups() reads an existing prop so it goes live additively.
- No pluggable PIP interface was introduced. The ticket says not to pre-design one; the C0 bundled policies reference only role×action×resource.type, so no C1/C2 resolvers fire. Don't add a PIP abstraction without a concrete consumer.
- Entity model: principal entity (CCF::User / CCF::Agent, id = subject.ID/email) is made a member (Cedar `in`) of CCF::Role::"<role>" entities built per-request from static assignments. Resources are CCF::<PascalCase> using the SAME cedarEntityName mapping + CCF namespace as `ccf authz export --format=cedar`, so bundled policies, exported schema and operator .cedar files all name the same entities.
- Agents get the `agent` role by default (zero-config ingest), overridable via authz-roles.yaml `agents:`. normalize() defaults empty -> "agent".
- Operator .cedar files (CCF_AUTHZ_CEDAR_POLICY_DIR) AUGMENT the bundled roles (added under op:<file>:<id> ids); they don't replace them. Cedar forbid>permit lets operators carve exceptions, incl. out of admin.
- Startup validation: role names in authz-roles.yaml are validated against the manifest (unknown -> fatal); missing assignment file is tolerated (users denied, agents still work, warn); malformed/strict-unknown-key file -> fatal. Fail-fast over silently denying everyone.
- Dependency: github.com/cedar-policy/cedar-go v1.8.0 (+ golang.org/x/exp transitive). cedar.Authorize is a pure function (no DB/network) so the cedar driver never returns ErrUnavailable and fail_mode never affects it.

lisa-design:
BCH-1328 — Native user groups (membership model + API + group PIP). Decisions:

- Models (internal/service/relational/groups.go): UserGroup->ccf_groups, UserGroupMembership->ccf_user_groups, SSOGroupMapping->ccf_sso_group_mappings. The Go type is UserGroup (not Group) ON PURPOSE: relational.Group already exists (OSCAL catalog group) — do not rename to Group.
- uuid/text discipline: ccf_groups.id is uuid; group_id/user_id join columns are text. All membership lookups use a TWO-STEP string `IN` query (ids first, then rows), never a column-to-column `uuid = text` join, which Postgres rejects (same hazard as the migrator's filter_controls cast). GroupNamesForUser, ListMembers, attachGroups all follow this.
- Group PIP (internal/authz/pip.go, dbGroupResolver = the design's resolveGroups): subject.groups = native CCF memberships ∪ SSO link groups, SSO groups translated via ccf_sso_group_mappings (unmapped pass through raw). Case-insensitive dedup (native spelling, resolved first, wins), sorted case-sensitively. Resolves the user by the user_uuid claim when present, else by email.
- resolvingPDP decorator is wired in authz.Open ONLY for non-builtin drivers (Cedar/AuthZen expect subject.groups pre-resolved — per BCH-1316). It sits OUTSIDE the decision cache so a membership change yields a new cache key -> fresh decision. The builtin driver is intentionally SKIPPED to avoid a per-request membership read on hot non-admin routes (evidence ingest); builtin resolves groups itself, lazily, only on the admin path.
- builtin.evaluateAdmin: native groups are a FALLBACK — queried only when the SSO link groups don't already satisfy RequiredAdminGroups. This keeps the common admin-allow at its prior 2-query cost (preserves TestBuiltinEvaluationsMemoizesAdmin's <=2 assertion) and is purely additive (native can only grant). A native-group query error degrades to a clean deny (logged), never a 500. The non-SSO "super admin" bypass and the SSO-disabled allow are UNCHANGED — those are separate interim behaviors, out of scope for BCH-1328.
- SSO->native mapping affects subject.groups (the resolver) only. The builtin admin check uses raw SSO link groups ∪ native memberships (mappings NOT applied there) — a deliberate scope boundary to keep the legacy admin path minimal.
- Freshness posture (ticket asks to decide): groups resolved per-request, NOT baked into the ~24h JWT, so revocation is timely; the only staleness window is the optional decision cache TTL (off by default), so admin-sensitive checks are always fresh by default.
- Admin API (internal/api/handler/groups.go) mounts under /api/admin/groups behind the existing RequireAdminGroups gate (the `admin` manifest resource). No new manifest resource was added. Membership is surfaced on the admin user views via userResponse.Groups.
